		{"id":"865236c05b2a2bab","type":"text","text":"# Grave Butterflies\n\nA grave is covered with small black pebbles. A butterfly sits on the stones. It looks as if it is there to die, and turn into a pebble itself.\n\n![Grave Butterflies](2026-06-02-grave-butterfly.jpeg)","x":-460,"y":200,"width":380,"height":830},
		{"id":"f01a802c7ce04d08","type":"text","text":"","x":-120,"y":1400,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"4b04caf933763e09","type":"text","text":"*List of stories collected during interviews and observations at the Cimetière des Rois.*","x":20,"y":-220,"width":380,"height":85,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"918ccc9fb9741241","type":"text","text":"# Rare Moss\n\nRare types of moss grow on graves. A form of new life, a new ecosystem born from the dead. These mosses are protected. Graves are not allowed to be cleaned (except by families). These rare mosses grow because the material of the graves is not native.\n\n![](2026-06-02-rare-moss.jpeg)","x":210,"y":460,"width":380,"height":902}
